{"kind":"request","text":"Demote the MCP shared-identity refusal to a warning, and correct its stated rationale. Hugh ruled in conversation on 2026-08-10 that the refusal is the wrong decision: multiple sessions of one actor are simply that — one actor — and neither stated justification survives contact with the rest of the system.\n\nEvidence (verified at 88b51864; re-verify at the implementation head): requireSoleIdentity (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:867-909) hard-refuses attach when the actor's presence label is already live, citing two rationales that the codebase itself contradicts. (1) The different-agent worry is backwards: independence is enforced by fingerprint, not spelling — cmd/gs/main.go:595 refuses a verdict whose signer signed the artifact, and the fold classifies same-fingerprint review as IndependenceSelfReview (internal/workroom/fold.go:149) — so two sessions sharing a key cannot spoof the review gate; their cross-reviews already carry no force. Definitionally, two sessions of one actor are not a different agent, so there is no independence between them to protect. (2) The attribution worry asserts instance-level semantics the record format deliberately rejects: durable records carry an actor fingerprint and no session field (internal/kernel, internal/intent — sessions exist only in the ephemeral nexus layer), so \"this actor did this\" remains exactly true under concurrent sessions. The hard refusal meanwhile blocks legitimate patterns: one person on two devices, an actor with planner and worker sessions, and crash-restart within the 30-second presence lease.\n\nWhat remains worth keeping is the early notice for the one real deployment mistake — two independent automated loops under one identity expecting their cross-reviews to count — plus coordination noise (duplicate claims, presence lease clobbering). Those justify a printed warning, not a refusal.\n\nDirection: in requireSoleIdentity, replace the sharedIdentityError refusal with a stderr warning (and/or a warning field on whoami) along the lines of: identity X is live in N other session(s); reviews between your own sessions carry no independent force, and concurrent sessions may race on claims and presence. Rewrite the function comment to the corrected rationale above. Keep the degraded-open behavior when the resident is unreachable. Keep the nexus-side session-to-actor binding (internal/nexus/nexus.go:242) exactly as is — one session signing as two actors is a different thing and stays refused. Update the affected docs page (docs/reference/mcp/whoami.md or wherever the refusal is documented) and any test that pins the refusal.","body":{"conditions":"Attaching a second session under a live identity succeeds with a visible warning naming the session count and the no-independent-force consequence; the sharedIdentityError refusal path is gone; the nexus session-to-actor binding refusal is unchanged; the function comment and docs state the corrected rationale (fingerprint-enforced independence at the fold, actor-level attribution in durable records); tests updated including the crash-restart-within-lease case now succeeding; make test and make race pass; implemented on a request/ branch, reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","decided_by":"hugh, in conversation, 2026-08-10","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main 88b51864; re-verify citations at the implementation head"}}